"\<Hence, the GGMT Lemma can be applied to this component.  Next, we look at the third component. We want to show that the spectral gap between -1/2 and the second eigenvalue is bigger than 1/2. For this,  we want to apply Proposition 3.1, which in turn requires to first shift the potential by 1/2, as as this turns the negative eigenvalue -\\frac12 into the  ground state eigenvalue 0. Then, we show that $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_2$ are big enough, while $\\beta $ is small enough such that the requirements of that Proposition are satisfies.  First we  establish that estimate $\\alpha_2>7/10. For this we need to look at V103eeff[r]-1/5.\>",
"Text",CellChangeTimes -> {{3.9965714013626423`*^9,3.9965715325559616`*^9},{3.9968102269183064`*^9,3.9968102311290054`*^9},{3.9968105310775967`*^9,3.9968105668840485`*^9},{4.003219736890028`*^9,4.0032197398030853`*^9},{4.003219805361458`*^9,4.003219805965994`*^9},{4.003225771410656`*^9,4.003225878547716`*^9},{4.003227117179981`*^9,4.0032271494925575`*^9}},
